This is the help documentation for the govax VAX emulator.  Topic sections are
marked by a "$" delimiter.  This is followed by as many tokens of the help
command as were parsed, separated by commas.  Note that if the token is 
less than four characters long, it must be blank padded.  For example, 
"HELP SET TRACE" would resolve to locating the marker "$SET ,TRAC".  The 
"$" must be the first character of the line, and the line can only contain 
the keyword match string.  The text is displayed until the next "$" marker 
is read, or the end of the file is found.

The only exception is if you have more than one possible key tag in a row 
for the same block of text.  For example, "HELP RUN" and "HELP GO" might 
both be tags for the same command if "GO" is a synonym for "RUN". 

$SHOW,LOGI
$SH  ,LOGI
$SHOW,LOG 
$SH  ,LOG 
    The SHOW LOGICALS command displays VMS logical names stored in the
    console for use by VMS programs.
    
    Syntax:
    
         SHOW LOGICAL [/TABLE=<tablename>]  [logicalname]

    Parameters:
    
        /TABLE=<tablename>
        
            The name of the logical name table to display names from.  If
            omitted, then all tables are searched/displayed.
        
        logicalname
        
            The name of the logical name to display the value of.  If
            omitted, then all names in the specified table are shown.
            If both the name and table name are omitted then all
            logical names are displayed.

$SHOW,DEVI
$SH  ,DEVI
$SHOW,DEV 
$SH  ,DEV 
    The SHOW DEVICE command displays information about a pseudodevice
    attached to the VMS system.  These devices are emulated via VMS
    system service calls that are managed by the emulator when a full
    version of VMS is not running.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW DEVICE [/FULL] [name]
    
    Parameter:
    
        name
        
            The name of a device to display.  If omitted then all
            known psueodevices are displayed.
        
        /FULL
        
            If this qualifier is present then all information about
            the device (essentially the device control block contents)
            is displayed.
 
$CLEA,BREA,/INS
$CLEA,BR  ,/INS
    The CLEAR BREAK/INSTRUCTION command clears an instruction breakpoint.
    Subsequent execution of the instruction will not result in a break
    operation.  The instruction to be cleared is identified by opcode
    name.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR BREAKPOINT/INSTRUCTION opcode

        CLEAR BREAKPOINT/INSTRUCTION/ALL

    Parameter:

        opcode    The mnemonic name for the instruction, such as MOVL
                  or INSQTI.  If you specifiy /ALL instead of an opcode
                  name then all instruction breakpoints are removed.

           
$CLEA,PROF
    The CLEAR PROFILE command zeros out the profile counters for each
    VAX instruction.  See the SHOW INSTRUCTION PROFILE command for 
    information on how instruction profiles are used.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR PROFILE

$SHOW,INST
$SH  ,INST
    The SHOW INSTRUCTION command prints a reminder of the instruction
    opcode values.  It only displays the instructions that are implemented
    in this version of the emulator.  The SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/ALL command
    displays additional information about each instructions operands, if
    any.

    Syntax:

        SHOW INSTRUCTIONS

        SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/ALL

$SHOW,INST,/UNI
$SHOW,UNIM,INST
$SH  ,INST,/UNI
$SH  ,UNIM,INST
    The SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/UNIMPLEMENTED command displays a table of the
    VAX instructions that are *not* currently implemented in the current
    version of govax.  Use of these instructions results in a reserved
    opcode fault.

    Syntax: 

        SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/UNIMPLEMENTED

$SHOW,INST,/PRO
$SH  ,INST,/PRO
    The SHOW INSTRUCTION/PROFILE command displays the number of times
    each VAX instruction has been executed.  This can be used to determine
    where a program is spending it's time, based on the cost of each type
    of instruction.

    You can reset the counters to zero with the CLEAR PROFILE command.

    Syntax:

         SHOW INSTRUCTION/PROFILE

$SHOW,INST,/MOD
$SH  ,INST,/MOD
    The SHOW INSTRUCTION/MODE command displays the number of times each VAX
    instruction mode has been executed.  This can be used to determine
    what addressing modes are used, etc..

    You can reset the counters to zero with the CLEAR PROFILE command.

    Syntax:

         SHOW INSTRUCTION/MODE

$CLEA,MEMO
$CLEA,MEM
$ZERO
    The CLEAR MEMORY command (or ZERO command, which is a synonym) is used
    to zero out the virtual VAX's memory.  All physical memory is cleared,
    which implicitly zeros out virtual memory.  When a CLEAR MEMORY command
    is given, it also turns virtual memory off, since the page tables will 
    have been zeroed.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR MEMORY

$CLEA,SYM ,/ALL
$CLEA,SYMB,/ALL
    The CLEAR SYMBOLS/ALL command deletes all symbols defined so far by the
    user or assembler, and releases the storage occupied by them.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR SYMBOL/ALL

$CLEA,SYM 
$CLEA,SYMB
    The CLEAR SYMBOL command deletes a single symbol definition and releases
    the storage occupied by the symbol.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR SYMBOL <symbol-name>

    Parameters:

        <symbol-name>   The symbol to be deleted.  It can be enclosed in
                        quotes but the quotes are optional.

    Examples:

        CLEAR SYMBOL MAIN_ENTRY

$CLEA,INT ,/ALL
$CLEA,INTE,/ALL
    The CLEAR INTERRUPTS/ALL command clears all pending interrupts that the
    processor has queued up.  This will prevent them from being delivered.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR INTERRUPTS/ALL

$CLEA,INT 
$CLEA,INTE
    The CLEAR INTERRUPT command is used to remove a specific interrupt from
    the pending interrupt queue on the processor.  Interrupts may be pending
    because they are waiting for a lower IPL to dispatch, or because they 
    are waiting for a delayed delivery to simulate hardware latency.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR INTERRUPT <code>

    Parameter:

        <code>     This is the interrupt code to clear.  If there is more
                   than one pending interrupt for this code, all the pending
                   interrupts are removed.

$CLEA,BR  ,/ALL
$CLEA,BREA,/ALL
    The CLEAR BREAKPOINT/ALL command is used to clear all breakpoints and
    release the storage occupied by their definition.  Subsequent execution
    of the breakpoint locations or incurring the named fault/exception (in
    the case of /FAULT breakpoints) will not cause a breakpoint halt of the
    processor.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR BREAKPOINT/ALL

$CLEA,BR  
$CLEA,BREA
    The CLEAR BREAKPOINT command is used to clear a specific breakpoint and
    release storage occupied by it's definition.  Subsequent execution of
    the breakpoint location will not caus a breakpoint halt of the processor.
    The command can also be used to clear a breakpoint on a fault as opposed
    to an instruction address by using the /FAULT qualifier, or clear a
    breakpoint when a specific instruction is executed with the /INSTRUCTION
    qualifier.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR BREAKPOINT  <address>

                  or

        CLEAR BREAKPOINT /FAULT <code>

    Parameters:

        <address>       The breakpoint address.  This can be a numeric value
                        in the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol enclosed
                        in single quotes.

        <code>          When the command includes the /FAULT qualifier, the
                        parameter is the fault code instead of an address.

$CLEA
    The CLEAR command is used to clear various attributes of the virtual VAX
    or the console processor.  See the help for

        CLEAR MEMORY
        CLEAR BREAKPOINT
        CLEAR BREAKPOINT/FAULT
        CLEAR BREAKPOINT/INSTRUCTION
        CLEAR INTERRUPT
        CLEAR PROFILE
        CLEAR STRINGS
        CLEAR SYMBOL
        CLEAR TB

$CLEA,TB  

    The CLEAR TB command clears the virtual memory translation buffer.  It
    also resets the counters used to track translation buffer use.  See
    SHOW TB for more information.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR TB

$DISA
$EXAM,/INS
    The EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION command (or it's alias DISASSEMBLE) is used to 
    display the contents of the virtual VAX memory formatted as executable 
    instructions.

    Syntax:

        EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION <start-addr> [<end-addr>]

    Parameters:

        <start-addr>    The starting address.  This can be a numeric value in
                        the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol enclosed in
                        single quotes.

        <end-addr>      The ending address of instructions to disassemble.
                        If not given, then one instruction is disassembled.

    Examples:

        EXAM/INSTR MAIN

        EXAM/INSTR 0200 0210

$EXAM
$EX  
    The EXAMINE command is used to display the contents of memory in the
    virtual VAX processor  If virtual memory is enabled, the display is
    of virtual storage, otherwise it is physical storage.

    Syntax:

        EXAMINE [<type>] <start-address> [<end-address>]

    Parameters:

        <type>          The type of data to be displayed from the memory.
                        Valid values are /ASCII, /BYTE, /WORD, or /LONG. You
                        can also display floating values with /F_FLOAT or
                        /D_FLOAT types. If omitted, then LONG is assumed.
                        
                        The /ASCII type can be followed by a subtype of
                        /DESCRIPTOR, /COUNTED, or /ZERO to reflect a
                        descriptor string, counted ASCII string, or null
                        terminated string.

        <start-address> The starting address.  This can be a numeric value in
                        the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol enclosed in
                        single quotes.

        <end-address>   The ending address.  If omitted, it defaults to the
                        same value as the starting address.  The end address
                        must be greater than or equal to the start address.


    Examples:

        EXAMINE MYDATA 

        EXAMINE /WORD 0200 0220

        EXAMINE /ASCII /DESCRIPTOR 300
        
        EXAMINE /D_FLOAT 200

$EXAM,REGS
$EXAM,REG 
$EXAM,REGI
    The EXAMINE REGISTER command displays the contents of all the virtual VAX
    registers formatted as longwords, plus the state of the PSL register.

    Syntax:
    
        EXAMINE REGISTERS

$INIT

    The INITIALIZE command performs one of two unrelated operations,
    selected by a required qualifier -- INITIALIZE (or its unambiguous
    4-letter abbreviation, INIT) with no qualifier at all is an error.

        INITIALIZE/VAX          (Re-)configures the amount of physical
                                 RAM the virtual VAX is running with.

        INITIALIZE/CONTAINER    Formats a new, empty ODS-2 disk-image
                                 container file for use with MOUNT.

    See the help for INITIALIZE/VAX and INITIALIZE/CONTAINER for the
    syntax of each.

$INIT,/VAX

    The INITIALIZE/VAX command (re-)initializes the virtual VAX, and
    specifies the amount of physical RAM the machine is configured with.
    This storage is allocated by the govax process from its own address
    space.

    Syntax:

        INITIALIZE/VAX <pages>

    Parameter:

        <pages>         The number of physical pages of VAX memory that the
                        virtual machine is configured with.  Each page is
                        512 bytes, so a page count of 1024 would require a
                        half-megabyte virtual machine, for example.

    Example:

        INITIALIZE/VAX 8192   ; Create a 4mb virtual VAX

    See also the help for INITIALIZE and INITIALIZE/CONTAINER.

$INIT,/CON

    The INITIALIZE/CONTAINER command formats a new, empty ODS-2 disk-image
    container file -- a real Files-11 volume, compatible with real VAX/VMS
    and other emulators such as simh.  The new container is not mounted;
    use MOUNT afterward to attach it to a device before using it.

    Syntax:

        INITIALIZE/CONTAINER <file> <size> [label] [/CLUSTER=<n>]

    Parameters:

        <file>          The path to the new disk-image container file on
                        the native file system.  If the native file system
                        is case sensitive, or the name contains embedded
                        blanks, it should be enclosed in quotes to preserve
                        case and spacing.

        <size>          The size of the new volume, in 512-byte blocks.

        [label]         The volume label to record in the container's home
                        block.  If omitted, the volume is labeled NONAME.

    Qualifier:

        /CLUSTER=<n>    The number of blocks per storage cluster.  If
                        omitted, the volume uses 1-block clusters.

    Example:

        INITIALIZE/CONTAINER "newdisk.dsk" 20000 WORK01

    See also the help for INITIALIZE, INITIALIZE/VAX, and MOUNT.

$QUIT

    The QUIT command terminates the console processor.  As long as there are
    no pending errors or signals to be processed, the emulator application
    quits.  If there are pending errors, etc. to be displayed, these are
    displayed on the console and the user must manually select the "Quit"
    menu item from the "File" menu.

$LOAD

    The LOAD command reads the contents of a saved memory image file
    into the current virtual VAX.  The LOAD command restores the contents
    of memory and the registers.  It does not restore saved symbols,
    breakpoints, or other console-specific context.   Also see the help
    for the LOAD/ROM command is used to load a ROM image into the console 
    ROM memory, and LOAD/NVRAM to load non-volatile RAM images.

    Syntax:

        LOAD    <file-name>

        
        
    Parameter:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "Saved.VAX"
                        is assumed.
        
        
$LOAD,/ROM

    The LOAD/ROM command reads a ROM file into the special memory area
    in the virtual VAX physical address space for a console ROM.  This
    ROM file can be a text file containing the console EXAM command
    output of the console memory, or a binary image created with the
    SAVE/ROM command.

    Syntax:


        LOAD/ROM [/BASE=<base-addr>] [/SIZE=<size-in-k>] <filename>
        
            or
        
        LOAD/ROM/NOERROR
        
        
    Parameters:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "default.rom"
                        is assumed.
        
        <base-addr>     When loading a ROM image, this specifies
                        the base physical address of the ROM image
                        to use.  If ommitted, 20040000 is assumed.
        
        <size-in-k>     This is the size of the ROM memory in kilobytes.
                        If ommitted, the default is ^d256 for a 256K ROM.

        Note that /BASE and /SIZE cannot be used if the ROM file being
        read is a ROM image created by SAVE/ROM; this has a fixed address
        and size associated with it.
        
        The second example (using the /NOERROR qualifier) indicates that if
        the default ROM file cannot be found then no error is displayed.  This
        allows the LOAD/ROM/NOERROR command to be placed in the initialization
        file, and not interfere with startup if a ROM file is not found.
$LOAD,/NVR

    The LOAD/NVRAM command reads a non-volatile RAM file into the special 
    memory area in the virtual VAX physical address space for the NVRAM.
    The NVRAM area exists on some VAX systems to contain console boot data,
    etc. and is used by the console ROM on those systems.  The NVRAM file
    is created with the SAVE/NVRAM command after INIT/NVRAM has first
    allocated it.
    

    Syntax:


        LOAD/NVRAM  <filename>
        
            or
        
        LOAD/NVRAM/NOERROR
        
        
    Parameters:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "default.nvram"
                        is assumed.
        
        
        The second example (using the /NOERROR qualifier) indicates that if
        the default NVRAM file cannot be found then no error is displayed.  This
        allows the LOAD/NVRAM/NOERROR command to be placed in the initialization
        file, and not interfere with startup if a NVRAM file is not found.
$INIT,/NVR

    The INIT/NVRAM command creates an empty non-volatile RAM area for use with
    a console ROM file.  The console ROM should contain code to initialize the
    NVRAM to a consistent state.  Use the SAVE/NVRAM command to save the resulting
    NVRAM file.  
    

    Syntax:


        INIT/NVRAM [/BASE=<base-addr>] [/SIZE=<size-in-k>]
                
        
    Parameters:

        
        <base-addr>     When creating an NVRAM area, this specifies
                        the base physical address of the NVRAM
                        to use.  If ommitted, 20080000 is assumed.
        
        <size-in-k>     This is the size of the NVRAM memory in kilobytes.
                        If ommitted, the default is 1 for a 1K NVRAM.

$SAVE

    The SAVE command writes the current memory contents to a binary file.
    This file can later be reloaded with the LOAD command.  For help on
    saving the entire machine state to a text file, see the help on
    SAVE/TEXT.  You can also use the SAVE/ROM command to save a ROM image.

    Note that the memory image for the SAVE command is not just a file
    image of the memory.  It contains a header of approxamately 1K of
    data describing the state of the virtual VAX at the time the SAVE
    command is issued.  This is followed by zero or more page ranges,
    where the address and number of pages is stored followed by that
    many pages of data.  The SAVE command only saves non-zero pages.
    
    Syntax:

        SAVE  <file-path>

    Parameter:

        <file-name>     This is the name of the file that the memory
                        image is saved to.  If omitted, the default
                        name of "Saved.VAX" is assumed.

$SAVE,/TEX

    The SAVE/TEXT command writes the entire state of the virtual VAX to a
    text file.  This text file can be re-read by using the INCLUDE
    command.

    Syntax:

        SAVE/TEXT <file-path>

    Parameter:

        <file-path>     This is the name of the file where the emulator
                        state is to be saved.  The name can be enclosed in
                        quotation marks if it contains imbedded blanks.
                        The file will be overwritten if it already exists.
    
    Example:

        SAVE/TEXT "myvax.status"

$@   
$INCL

    The INCLUDE command (which can be abbreviated to "@") is used to read 
    an external text file as a set of commands, as if they were typed by 
    the user at the console.  It can be used to store a set of often-
    repeated commands, for example.  It can also be used to retrieve a 
    stored VAX state created by the SAVE TEXT command, since the saved 
    state can be expressed as a series of commands that will re-create 
    the virtual VAX.
    
    Syntax:
    
        INCLUDE <file-path>
    
    
    Parameter:
    
        <file-path>     This is the name of the text file to read.  It must
                        already exist or an error occurs.
    
    Example:
    
        INCLUDE MYVAX.STATUS


$GO  

    The GO command is used to invoke the emulator and begin execution of a stream 
    of instructions in the virtual VAX.  The command begins execution at the current
    value of the PC register, and can optionally specify a starting address.
    
    Syntax:
    
        GO [<address>]
    
    Parameters:
    
        <address>       The starting address.  This can be a numeric value in
                        the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol enclosed in
                        single quotes.
                        
    
    Examples:
    
        GO
        
        GO 00000200
        
        GO ENTRY_PT

$ASM ,PSEU,SHIM
$ASSE,PSEU,SHIM
    The .SHIM pseudo-operand creates a software shim entry.  This is used in
    the micro-kernel to map runtime library vector points to support routines
    in the micro-kernel.  Some of these support routines are implemented in
    the micro-kernel itself, and some transfer control to native runtime code
    in the govax emulator.
    
    Syntax:
    
        .SHIM   <entry-name>, [<runtime-code>], <runtime-library>, <offset>
    
    Parameters:
    
        <entry-name>        This is the name of the entry point that supports
                            the runtime function.  It may be the name of an
                            already-defined entry point in the micro-kernel,
                            or a shim stub will be generated by default with
                            this name.
        
        <runtime-code>      If specified, this is the dispatch code for the
                            integrated runtime library in the native emulator
                            code.  See the files shim.c and rtl.c for more
                            information.  If omitted or zero, then a shim
                            stub is not create, and entry-name is assumed to
                            specify a real VAX support routine in the micro-
                            kernel.
        
        <runtime-library>   The name of the runtime library that this is an
                            entry point shim for, such as LIBRTL or DECC$SHR.
                            Do not include the ".EXE" suffix.  This name must
                            match the name of a sharable library list entry
                            in an image for the shim to be bound to the image.
        
        <offset>            This is the offset into the sharable image library
                            where the routine resides.  Usually this is an
                            offset near the start of the actual sharable image
                            code that is a jump vector to the "real" routine.
                            In any case, this offset value (a longword) must
                            exactly match the offset in the image to be bound.
    
    Example:
    
        .SHIM   LIB$PUT_OUTPUT, 0, LIBRTL, 0478
        
        This indicates that when an image is loaded that includes a sharable
        image reference to LIBRTL+0478, then those references will be remapped
        to the LIB$PUT_OUTPUT routine.  Because the runtime library dispatch 
        code is zero, the routine must be a VAX routine, already assembled in 
        memory.
        
        
        .SHIM   STR$UPCASE, 2, LIBRTL, 0A70
        
        This indicates that when an image is loaded with a reference to 
        LIBRTL+0A70, it will call a shim stub named STR$UPCASE.  The shim stub 
        is created as part of the assembly of the .SHIM instruction.  This stub
        will use the special XFC instruction to transfer control to the RTL 
        support routine for code 2.  See shim.c and rtl.c for more information.
        
$RUN 
$RUN ,/DEB
    The RUN command loads a VAX/VMS image into memory, and executes it.  This
    command assumes that a VMINIT has been run, and the standard micro-kernel
    (kernel.asm) has been loaded and initialized.  
    
    The command will load a valid, normal VAX/VMS image into memory.  References
    to sharable images must be resolved using "software shims".  These are stubs
    that map sharable image vector slots to routines in memory, typically supplied
    by the micro-kernel.  Some shims may redirect control to native runtime code
    via a special XFC instruction.  See the help on ASM PSEUDO SHIM or SHOW SHIM
    for more information.
    
    If the image has a transfer address, then the image is started at it's user
    transfer address.  If there is no transfer address, the image is loaded but
    not executed.  
    
    If the optional /DEBUG qualifier is used, then the image is loaded but not
    started automatically.  You must use a CALL command to the symbol MAIN (created
    by the loader) to start the loaded program.
    
    Syntax:
    
        RUN [/DEBUG] <image-name>
    
    Parameter:
    
        <image-name>    This is the name of an image file on the native system.
                        This can be created on a VAX/VMS system, and then copied
                        to the native system where the emulator runs via FTP or
                        other process.  Note that the file must be transfered in
                        binary mode.  
                        
                        If the native file system is case sensitive, the name may 
                        need to be enclosed in quotes to preserve case.
                        
                        The image is mapped into memory and executed.

$CALL
    The CALL command is used to invoke the emulator and begin execution of a
    stream of instructions in the virtual VAX.  The command begins execution
    at the address supplied in the command by invoking it with a CALLS
    procedure call.  The command can pass a varying number of longword 
    parameters to the procedure if desired.

    The return value of the procedure is available in R0, and is also copied
    to the variable $STATUS.
    
    Syntax:
    
        CALL <address> [ ( arg1 [, arg2...] ) ]
    
    Parameters:
    
        <address>       The starting address.  This can be a numeric value in
                        the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol.  Because
                        the code is invoked with a CALLS instruction, this
                        must be the address of an entry mask word.  The code
                        stream should end with a RET instruction.
        
        <argn>          Up to fifteen arguments can be supplied to the
                        routine being called.  The CALL command simulates a
                        CALLS instruction, so a stack frame is created with
                        the argument list on the stack.  The arguments are
                        pushed as longwords on the stack and must all be
                        integers.  If arguments are passed they must be
                        enclosed in parenthesis.  If more than one argument
                        is present, separate them with commas.
                        
    
    Examples:
    
        CALL 0200
        
        CALL SUM( ^D35, ^D88 )

$CALL,/STE
    The CALL/STEP command is used to invoke the emulator and begin execution of a
    stream of instructions in the virtual VAX.  This is similar to the function of
    the CALL command, except that the /STEP qualifier indicates that the console
    should only step to the first instruction.  The command begins execution
    at the address supplied in the command by invoking it with a CALLS
    procedure call.  The command can pass a varying number of longword 
    parameters to the procedure if desired.  However, only the first instruction
    of the function is executed.  You must use a GO or STEP command to continue
    execution.

    
    Syntax:
    
        CALL/STEP <address> [ ( arg1 [, arg2...] ) ]
    
    Parameters:
    
        <address>       The starting address.  This can be a numeric value in
                        the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol.  Because
                        the code is invoked with a CALLS instruction, this
                        must be the address of an entry mask word.  The code
                        stream should end with a RET instruction.
        
        <argn>          Up to fifteen arguments can be supplied to the
                        routine being called.  The CALL command simulates a
                        CALLS instruction, so a stack frame is created with
                        the argument list on the stack.  The arguments are
                        pushed as longwords on the stack and must all be
                        integers.  If arguments are passed they must be
                        enclosed in parenthesis.  If more than one argument
                        is present, separate them with commas.
                        
    
    Examples:
    
        CALL 0200
        
        CALL SUM( ^D35, ^D88 )


$STEP,/INT
$ST  ,/INT
$STEP,/IN 
$ST  ,/IN 
    The STEP/INTO modifies the STEP command.  If the next intruction is a
    CALL or JSB instruction, then the STEP command begins execution with the
    first instruction in the procedure or subroutine.  This overrides the
    default STEP behavior.

    If the next instruction is not a CALL or JSB then the instruction STEPs
    normally. 

    See the help on the STEP command for more information on how STEP works.
$STEP,/OVE
$ST  ,/OVE
    The STEP/OVER modifies the STEP command.  If the next intruction is a
    CALL or JSB instruction, then the STEP command sets a temporary breakpoint
    on the instruction following the CALL or JSB and lets the procedure run
    normally.  This overrides the default STEP behavior.
    
    If the next instruction is not a CALL or JSB then the instruction STEPs
    normally. 

    See the help on the STEP command for more information on how STEP works.
$STEP,/RET
$ST  ,/RET
    The STEP/RETURN modifies the STEP command.  A temporary breakpoint is set
    a the return address of the current procedure and execution continues.  
    When the procedure returns, the breakpoint is triggered.  This allows you
    to step "out" of the current procedure.  This overrides the default STEP
    behavior.
    
    See the help on the STEP command for more information on how STEP works.
$STEP
$ST  
$S

    The STEP command is used to execute a single instruction in the virtual
    VAX.  The command is executed at the current value of the PC register,
    or can optionally be specified using a starting address parameter.

    When an instruction is executed by the STEP command, the instruction that
    is executed is disassembled, regardless of the setting of the console
    disassembly mode.  See the help on SET DISASSEMBLE for more information.

    See information on STEP/OVER, STEP/INTO and STEP/RETURN for optional
    qualifiers to the STEP command.

    Syntax:

        STEP [<address>]

    Parameters:

        <address>       The starting address.  This can be a numeric value in
                        the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol enclosed in
                        single quotes.
                        
    
    Examples:
    
        STEP
        
        STEP  00000200
        
        STEP ENTRY_PT

$SET ,FAUL
$SE  ,FAUL
   The SET FAULT command sets the size of the fault buffer.  The argument is
   the number of faults that are tracked in the history buffer.  When the 
   buffer becomes full, old fault records are discarded to make room for new
   ones.  See the SHOW FAULT command for information on the display of stored
   faults and exceptions.
   
   When the SET FAULT command is issued, the fault buffer is cleared as a
   side effect of re-allocating a new buffer.

   Syntax:
   
       SET FAULT <size>
       
   Parameter:
   
       <size> 		The number of fault and exception entries stored in
                        the fault buffer.
    
   Example:
   
        SET FAULT 8
        

$SET 

    The SET command is used to set the state of information in the virtual
    VAX or the console processor.  See the help text on:

        SET ASSEMBLER
        SET BASE
        SET BREAK
        SET DEBUG
        SET DEFAULT
        SET DISASSEMBLER
        SET FAULT
        SET MODE
        SET PAGE
        SET PSL
        SET QUANTUM
        SET RADIX
        SET REGISTER
        SET SYMBOL
        SET TRACE
        SET VERIFY
        SET VM

$SET ,REGI
$SET ,REG 

    The SET command can be used to set a register value.  To do this, 
    name the register and value separated by an equals symbol.  The
    register can be a general purpose register such as R3 or a 
    privileged register such as SCBB.  To set a privileged register,
    the processor must be in kernel mode.

    Syntax:

        SET <register> = <value>

    Parameters:

       <register>     The register name.

       <value>        Any numeric expression.  The expression is
                      evaluated and the result stored in the register
                      named.

$SET ,SYMB
$SET ,SYM 

    The SET command can be used to set a symbol value.  Optionally, 
    you can specify attributes of the symbol to be set.  If the symbol
    name already exists, the command updates the value.  If the symbol
    does not already exist, it is created.

    Syntax:

        SET [qualifiers] <symbol> = <value>

    Parameters:

        <symbol>        The name of the symbol to set.  If the name
                        contains a "$" character then it is a system
                        symbol and goes in the system table.  Otherwise
                        it is a user symbol.

        <value>         Any expression.  The expression is evaluated and
                        the result stored in the symbol.

    Qualifiers:

        /PERMANENT      The symbol is marked permanent.  If it is in
                        the system symbol table it will not be deleted,
                        even if all other symbols are removed.

        /LABEL          The symbol is a label of a memory location, and
                        can be used during disassembly to replace a numberic
                        address value.

        /ENTRY          The symbol is an entry point of a two-byte entry
                        mask.

$SET ,DEBU

    The SET DEBUG command sets various debugging switches in the console
    or emulator.  To set the debug switch, use it's name shown below.  To
    turn off the switch, previx the name with NO.

    Syntax:

        SET DEBUG [NO]<name>

    Parameter:

        <name>   The name of a debugging switch.  The values currently
                 supported are:

                 DEBUG         Switch to native debugger if available.

                 VM            Enable debugging messages for virtual memory
                               translations.

                 TB            Enable debugging messages for translation
                               buffer caching.

                 SYMBOLS       Enable debugging messages for symbol
                               creation or resolution.

                 REGISTERS     Enable display of registers that change
                               value on a STEP command.

                 INTERRUPTS    Enable debugging messages for interrupts.

                 EXCEPTIONS    Enable debugging messages for exceptions.

                 IMAGES        Enabled SYS$IMGACT mapping when RUN
                               command issued for an image.

                 USERHALT      Enable HALT instruction in USER mode.

                 KEYBOARD      Debug console keyboard IO activity.

                 SERVICES      Debug P1 vector calls to VMS system services.

                 DCL           Debug DCL command line parsing in console.

                 CHM           Enable debug messages on CHMx or REI.

                 COMMAND       Display command line expansions after symbol
                               substitution in the console.

                 LOGICALS      Debug emulated VMS logical name support.

                 DEVICES       Debug emulated VMS device support.

                 LIBINIT       Enable LIB$INITIALIZE calls on image load.


$SET ,ASM 
$SET ,ASSE

    The SET ASSEMBLER command is used to control characteristics of the
    built-in instruction assembler in the console.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET ASSEMBLER <flag>
    
    Parameters:
    
            
        [NO]ADDRESS_PROMPT
        
            When set, this flag tells the pseudo-assembler to display
            the memory address in which the instruction(s) will be 
            written as part of the prompt for input.

        [NO]FORWARD_WARNINGS
        
            When set, this flag tells the assembler to issue a warning
            whenever the assembler finishes processing statements and
            there are unresolved forward references.  These symbols
            must be resolved before the console will allow a RUN
            statement to execute.

        [NO]BRANCH_DEST

            When set, disassembly of instructions will include a
            comment field that lists the numeric destinations of
            symbolic addresses.  This can make reading the disassembly
            somewhat easier.

        [NO]SYMBOLS

            When set, the disassembler will attempt to convert branch
            and address references back into symbolic names if there
            is a label or entry with a matching value.

        [NO]RESOLVE_TEMP

            When set, the assembler allows symbols prefixed with a "_"
            character to be considered "temporary" with the definition
            of a .ENTRY procedure.  When the end of the entry is
            assembled, the temporary names are qualified by the name of
            the entry that contains them, making them globally unique.

        [NO]SCOPENAMES

            When set, temporary symbols (prefixed with "_") are made
            global by qualifying them with the name of the ENTRY that
            contains them.  When clear, a unique integer number is
            generated instead to define the scope.  This makes the name
            of the symbol shorter (and easier to read in disassembly)
            but less clear which entry it belongs to.


$SET ,SYM
$SET ,SYMB

    The SET command can be used to set a symbol value.  The symbol value is
    then usable anywhere an address can be specified, in a console command
    or an assembler statement.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET <symbol> = <value>
    
    
    Parameters:
    
        <symbol>        The name of the symbol to be set.  Do not enclose the
                        symbol name in single quotes (that is used to mean
                        "resolve the symbol").
        
        <value>         Any numeric valid or a symbol value enclosed in single
                        quotes.  This can also be any integer expression.

    Examples:
    
        SET MAIN = 0200         ; Set MAIN to 0200 (in current RADIX)
        
        SET MAIN = INIT + 2     ; Set MAIN to value of INIT plus two

$SET ,REG 
$SET ,REGI

    The SET command can be used to set a virtual VAX machine register by
    name.  You can use any register name such as R0..R15 or AP, SP, FP,
    and PC.  You can also specify any of the privileged processor register
    names such as SIBB or KSP, or the PSL register.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET <register> = <value>

    
    Parameters:
    
        <register>      The name of the register to be set.  
        
        <value>         Any numeric valid or a symbol value enclosed in single
                        quotes.  It can also be any valid integer expression.

    Examples:
    
        SET R5 = 0200           ; Set R5 to 0200 (in current RADIX)
        
        SET AP = INIT           ; Set AP register to value of INIT

        SET SIBB = 0800         ; Set system info base register to 0800

$SET ,PSL 

    The SET PSL command is used to force settings of fields in the PSL, or
    of the entire PSL longword value.

    Syntax:

        SET PSL = <value>

        or

        SET PSL <field> = <fvalue>  [, <field> = <fvalue>...]

    Parameters:

        <value>    You can set the entire longword value in the PSL by
                   giving it a single value.  For example, 000003C0 will
                   reset the PSL to user mode, IPL 0, with no condition
                   flags set.

        <field>    You can set specific fields in the PSL by naming the
                   field.  The field names are CM, TP, FPD, IS, CUR_MOD, 
                   PRV_MOD, IPL, DV, FU, IV, T, N, Z, V, and C.  You can
                   specify more than one field, separate by commas if you
                   wish.

        <fvalue>   When setting a field value, the value must be in the
                   valid numeric range.  For example, the CUR_MOD field
                   is two bits wide, and contain values between 0 and 3.

    Exmaples:

        SET PSL = 000003C0         ; Reset to default user mode state

        SET PSL CUR_MOD=3, IPL=0   ; Resets just mode and IPL fields.

                
$SET ,VM  
$SET ,NOVM
$SET ,MAPE
$SET ,NOMA

    The SET VM command can be used to enable or disable virtual memory.  The
    command can also be expressed as SET MAPENABLE which is the name of the
    privileged register that stores this state information.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET [NO]VM
            
            -or-
        
        SET [NO]MAPENABLE

    Examples:
    
        SET VM          ; Turn on virtual memory mapping
        
        SET NOVM        ; Turn off virtual memory mapping
$SET ,BR  
$SET ,BREA

    The SET BREAK command specifies a breakpoint.  When the instruction
    located at the address specified in the breakpoint is about to be
    executed, the console will halt the processor.  This is used as a
    debugging tool.

    You can also use the /FAULT qualifier to identify a fault number
    that is used to trigger a breakpoint.  When the named exception
    is signalled, the breakpoint is taken and control returns to the
    console before the exception handler in the SCB is given control.
   
    You can also use the /INSTRUCTION qualifier, which identifies a
    specific opcode that, when executed, will cause a break operation
    to occur.  The instruction in this case is identified by it's
    mnemonic opcode name, such as MOVL or INSQTI.
 
    Syntax:
    
        SET BREAK <address> [ <count> ]
    
            or

        SET BREAK /FAULT <exception> 

            or

        SET BREAK /INSTRUCTION <opcode>


    Parameter:
    
        <address>       The instruction address.  This can be a numeric value
                        in the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol enclosed
                        in single quotes.  It can also be any valid integer
                        expression.

        <count>         The number of times to skip before taking the break
                        point.  If the value is missing or zero, then the
                        next time the instruction address is referenced in
                        instruction decoding, it will break.

        <exception>     If you use the /FAULT qualifier, the value is the
                        exception code number.  This is the offset into
                        the SCB for the exception.  For example, an access
                        violation is at offset 0020 into the SCB, so the
                        exception value is 20.

        <opcode>        If you use the /INSTRUCTION qualifier, the value
                        must be the mnemonic name of the instruction on
                        which to set the breakpoint.
    
    Examples:
    
        SET BREAK 0200
        
        SET BREAK MAIN

        SET BREAK /FAULT  EXC$CONWRITE   ; Use symbolic exception name

        SET BREAK /INSTR  ADDL3          ; Break on any ADDL3 instruction

$SET ,BASE

    The SET BASE command sets the base address from which assembled
    instructions are stored.  Typically, this is used in a SAVE text
    file to define the location where the next instructions are to 
    be placed.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET BASE <address>
    
    Parameter:

        <address>       The starting address.  This can be a numeric value in
                        the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol enclosed in
                        single quotes.
    Examples:
    
        SET BASE 0200
        
        SET BASE MYDATA

$SET ,VERI
$SET ,NOVE

    The SET VERIFY command determines whether the text file(s) read with the
    INCLUDE command are displayed on the console as they are processed.  For
    small text files this can be a useful debugging tool.  However, for large
    files (particularly large SAVE files) this may be too slow.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET [NO]VERIFY
    
    Examples:
    
        SET VERIFY
        
        SET NOVERIFY

$SET ,RAD 
$SET ,RADI

    The SET RADIX command is used to set the default radix (numeric base) of
    constant values that do not have an explicit radix indicator like X^ or
    D^.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET RADIX <base>

    Parameter:
    
        <base>          The specified radix.  Valid values are HEXADECIMAL
                        or DECIMAL.

    Examples:
    
        SET RADIX HEXADECIMAL       ; constant will be interpreted as hex
                                    ; numeric values as if the X^ prefix
                                    ; had been used.

$SET ,DISA
$SET ,NODI

    The SET DISASSEMBLER command enables dynamic disassembly of instructions
    as they are executed.  This is comparatively very slow but is useful for
    debugging instruction flow.
    
    Note that this command does not affect the use of the DISASM command for
    explicitly disassembling blocks of memory from the console.
    
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET [NO]DISASSEMBLER

$SET ,TRAC
$SET ,NOTR

    The SET TRACE command enables or disables the trace bit in the PSL of
    the virtual VAX.  When the trace bit is set, then a T-BIT fault is
    signalled after the execution of the next instruction.  This technique
    is used by VMS debuggers, for example, to single step code.  You can
    test the operation of the T-BIT fault by setting the trace bit with
    this instruction.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET [NO]TRACE

$SHOW,DEBU
$SH  ,DEBU

    The SHOW DEBUG command displays the settings of the internal debug
    switches.  These are set with the SET DEBUG command.  See the help
    for SET DEBUG for more information.

    Syntax:

        SHOW DEBUG

$SHOW,QUAN
$SH  ,QUAN

    The SHOW QUANTUM command displays the execution quantum the emulator
    guarantees before allowing VAX interrupts to be processed. This is
    expressed as a number of instructions; after that many instructions
    have been executed, the processes any queued interrupts.  A quantum
    of zero disables the quantum mechanism, and uses the realtime clock
    instead to signal when to handle interrupts. The use of quantum is
    largely to ensure that interrupt behavior is deterministic.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW QUANTUM

$SET ,QUAN

    The SET QUANTUM command sets the execution quantum the emulator
    guarantees before allowing VAX interrupts to be processed. This is
    expressed as a number of instructions; after that many instructions
    have been executed, the processes any queued interrupts.  A quantum
    of zero disables the quantum mechanism, and uses the realtime clock
    instead to signal when to handle interrupts. The use of quantum is
    largely to ensure that interrupt behavior is deterministic.
   
    Syntax:
    
        SET QUANTUM n
    
    Parameter:
    
        n       The number of instructions to be executed before the 
                emulator will process interrupts deterministically.

$SHOW,REG
$SHOW,REGI
$SH  ,REG
$SH  ,REGI

    The SHOW REGISTERS command is used to display the processor register
    set.  
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW REGISTERS
    
$SHOW,ASSE
$SH  ,ASSE
$SHOW,ASM 
$SH  ,ASM 

    The SHOW ASSEMBLER command displays settings for the built-in 
    instruction assembler.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW ASSEMBLER

$SHOW,BR  
$SHOW,BREA
$SH  ,BR  
$SH  ,BREA

    The SHOW BREAKPOINTS command displays the active breakpoints.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW BREAKPOINTS

$SHOW,BREA,/INS
$SH  ,BREA,/INS
$SHOW,BR  ,/INS
$SH  ,BR  ,/INS

    The SHOW BREAKPOINT/INSTRUCTION command displays breakpoints set
    when specific instructions are executed regardless of their
    address.

    Syntax:

        SHOW BREAKPOINTS/INSTRUCTION

$SH  ,PSL 
$SHOW,PSL 

    The SHOW PSL command displays the contents of the Processor Status
    Longword (PSL).  
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW PSL
    
    The PSL is displayed as a 32-bit hexadecimal value, and also as the
    individual fields of the PSL.  These fields are:
    
    C       carry bit
    V       overflow bit
    Z       zero value
    N       negative value
    T       trace pending
    IV      integer overflow
    FU      floating point underflow
    DV      divide-by-zero
    IPL     current interrupt priority level
    MOD     current mode (user, kernel, supervisor, executive)

$SH  ,CPU 
$SHOW,CPU 

    The SHOW CPU command displays the contents of the processor state. This
    includes the value of each of the processor registers, the privileged
    processor registers, and the PSL.  The size of physical memory is also
    displayed.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW CPU

$SHOW,BASE
$SH  ,BASE
$SHOW,DEPO
$SHOW,DEP
$SH  ,DEPO
$SH  ,DEP

    The SHOW BASE command displays the deposit base address.  This is the
    address set by SET BASE or the .BASE assembler directive.  This address
    is the next location where the assembler will write instructions or data
    by default.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW BASE

$SHOW,VM  
$SH  ,VM  
$SHOW,MAPE
$SH  ,MAPE
$SHOW,MEM 
$SH  ,MEM     
    The SHOW MEMORY command displays the size of the physical memory model
    used by the emulator, the state of the virtual memory mode of the
    processor, controlled by the privileged register MAPEN.  Also, if 
    virtual memory is enabled via the VMINIT command, it will display the 
    current page table information known to the processor.
    
    See the additional help on SHOW MEMORY STATISTICS for information on
    displaying memory use by the emulator (as opposed to the VAX).


    Syntax:
    
        SHOW MEMORY

$SHOW,VM  ,STAT
$SH  ,VM  ,STAT
$SHOW,MAPE,STAT
$SH  ,MAPE,STAT
$SHOW,MEM ,STAT
$SH  ,MEM ,STAT

    The SHOW MEMORY STATISTICS command will display information about the
    use of the native memory by the emulator.  The display shows how many 
    bytes have been allocated and freed, and which are for the emulator 
    memory model itself.  Other consumers of memory by govax include
    buffers for console I/O, and storage used for symbol tables, etc. in 
    the assembler.

    Syntax:

        SHOW VM STATISTICS

$SHOW,STAC
$SH  ,STAC

    The SHOW STACK command dumps the top of the active processor stack.  You
    can optionally specify the number of longwords to be dumped.  By default
    only the top longword is displayed.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW STACK <count>
    
    Parameter:
    
        count       The number of longwords to be displayed.  If you specify
                    a count greater than the size of the stack, the only the
                    valid stack longwords are displayed.

$SHOW,EXCE
$SH  ,EXCE

    The SHOW EXCEPTION command displays the most recent exception recorded
    by the processor.  If there has never been an exception, then a message
    is displayed showing that there is no exception data.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW EXCE

$SHOW,RADI
$SHOW,RAD 
$SH  ,RADI
$SH  ,RAD 

    The SHOW RADIX command displays the default radix being used by the
    console for input.  Valid values are DECIMAL or HEXADECIMAL, and can
    be set by the SET RADIX command or the .RADIX assember directive.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW RADIX

$SHOW,SYMB
$SHOW,SYM 
$SH  ,SYMB
$SH  ,SYM 

    The SHOW SYMBOL command can be used to display the value of a specific
    symbol in the symbol table.  You can also use the SHOW SYMBOL/ALL
    command to display all symbols, or the SHOW SYMBOL/UNRESOLVED command
    to display symbols that do not yet have an assigned value.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW SYMBOL <name>
    
    Parameter:
    
        name        The name of a symbol to be displayed.  If the symbol
                    does not exist then an error message is displayed.

$SHOW,SYMB,/ALL
$SHOW,SYM ,/ALL
$SH  ,SYMB,/ALL
$SH  ,SYM ,/ALL

    The SHOW SYMBOL/ALL command will display the settings of all user
    symbols, if there are any.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW SYMBOLS/ALL

$SHOW,SYMB,/UNR
$SHOW,SYM ,/UNR
$SH  ,SYMB,/UNR
$SH  ,SYM ,/UNR

    The SHOW SYMBOL/UNRESOLVED command displays all symbols that are 
    defined but whose value is not yet known.  These symbols are created
    by the assembler when a symbol is used in an instruction but the value
    of the symbol has not yet been defined.  These symbols must be defined
    before the console will begin executing code.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW SYMBOL/UNRESOLVED

$SHOW,TRAC
$SH  ,TRAC
    The SHOW TRACE command displays the console trace (dissassembler)
    flag.  When set, this flag causes the console to display each VAX
    instruction as it is executed.  This is very slow but is useful in
    debugging code streams.  When clear (the default) the no disassembly
    is included as part of instruction execution.
    
    Note that the setting of this flag does not affect the use of the
    DISASM command.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW TRACE

$SHOW,MEMO
$SHOW,MEM 
$SH  ,MEMO
$SH  ,MEM 
$SHOW,VM  
$SH  ,VM  

    The SHOW MEMORY command is used to display the status of the virtual
    and physical memory layout.  The display shows the amount of physical
    memory available to the virtual VAX.  If virtual memory is initialized,
    it also shows the P0, P1, and S0 region sizes, base and length register
    values, and PTE sizes.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW MEMORY

$SHOW,CALL
$SH  ,CALL
    The SHOW CALLS command displays the current contents of the stack
    frame array.  This is the list of call frames (implicitly a linked
    list starting with the current FP).  The frames are displayed until
    a frame is reached that was started by the console.

    Syntax:

        SHOW CALLS [<count>]

    Parameter:

        <count>    If give, this is the number of call frames to display
                   starting with the current frame.  For example, the
                   command SHOW CALLS 1 will display only the current
                   stored frame on the stack pointed to by the FP.

                   If the count is ommitted, then all call frames are
                   displayed.
$SHOW,ERRO
$SHOW,ERR 
$SH  ,ERRO
$SH  ,ERR 
    The SHOW ERROR command displays the message associated with a console
    error code.  If no code is given, it shows the message associated 
    with the last error code.

    Syntax:

        SHOW ERROR [<code>]

    Parameter:

        <code>     A numeric value or expression that reflects the code
                   you wish to see the message text for.  If ommitted,
                   the value of the symbol $STATUS which holds the 
                   status of the last non-successful console command.
$SHOW
$SH

    The SHOW command is used to display information about the virtual
    VAX or the console processor.  For additional SHOW commands, see
    the help for:
    
        SHOW ASSEMBLER
        SHOW BASE
        SHOW BREAKPOINTS
        SHOW CALLS
        SHOW CPU
        SHOW DEFAULT
        SHOW DEVICE
        SHOW DISASSEMBLER
        SHOW ERROR
        SHOW EXCEPTION
        SHOW INSTRUCTION
        SHOW INSTRUCTION/PROFILE
        SHOW INSTRUCTION/MODE
        SHOW INSTRUCTION/UNIMPLEMENTED
        SHOW LOGICAL
        SHOW MEMORY
        SHOW MODE
        SHOW PAGE
        SHOW PSL
        SHOW QUANTUM
        SHOW RADIX
        SHOW REGISTERS
        SHOW SCB
        SHOW SHIMS
        SHOW STACK
        SHOW STRINGS
        SHOW SYMBOL
        SHOW TB
        
    The SHOW command is also used to display the contents of a virtual
    VAX machine register.  This includes the general purpose registers
    R0-R15 as well as the PSL and any privileged processor register.

    Syntax:

        SHOW <register-name>

    
    Parameter:

        <register-name>     This is the name of the register whose contents
                            you wish to display.  This can be a general
                            purpose register (R0-R15), or one of the valid
                            synonyms like AP, FP, SP, or PC.  It can also
                            be a privileged register like SIBB or KSP.
                            
                            See the help for REGISTER NAMES for a complete
                            list of the register names allowed.

$SHOW,STRI
$SH  ,STRI
    The SHOW STRINGS command displays the string pool managed by the
    assembler.  This allows you to use quoted strings in expressions
    where an address value is normally used.  The assembler creates
    the string in a reserved area of memory (the string pool) and
    substibutes the address of a descriptor for the string in the
    expression.

    Syntax:

        SHOW STRINGS


    Usage:

        To use a string constant, place it in an assembly statement
        anywhere you would use an address expression.  For example,
        the following writes a string to the console using the
        Microkernel.

             PUSHL     I^#0A
             PUSHL     I^#0D
             PUSHL     I^#"This is a test"
             CALLS     #3, @#LIB$PUT_OUTPUT

        The use of the quoted string for the longword value causes a
        descriptor to be created for the string value (stored in a
        reserved area of S0 space defined by the CONSOLE$STRINGPOOL_BASE
        symbol) and the descriptor address is pushed on the stack.

        Note that the value use is _always_ a descriptor address, not
        the address of the string constant itself.

$CLEA,STRI
    The CLEAR STRINGS command resets the string pool.  This invalidates
    any string values used, so the command should only be used when
    clearing programs from memory, etc.   See the SHOW STRINGS command
    for information on the string pool.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR STRINGS

$SHOW,SCB 
$SH  ,SCB 

    The SHOW SCB command displays the currently active exception
    vector (system control block).  The base address of this memory
    is defined by the SCBB processor register.  The command will
    display the SCBB value (which is a physical address) and then 
    will display the addresses of the exception handlers declared
    in the SCB, if any.

    Syntax:

        SHOW SCB

$SHOW,PAGE
$SH  ,PAGE
$SHOW,PTE 
$SH  ,PTE 

    The SHOW PAGE (or SHOW PTE) command displays the virtual memory
    characteristics for a page of memory given an access type.  This
    command will not work unless virtual memory is turned on.

    Syntax:

        SHOW PAGE [<mode>] <address>  

    Parameters:

        <address>     Any exprssion representing a page of VAX virtual
                      memory.  The information displayed is for the 
                      page containing the specified address.

        <mode>        This must be /READ or /WRITE.  If not given, then
                      /READ is assumed.  This is used to determine if
                      the page is accessible in the current mode.

$SET ,PAGE
$SET ,PTE 

    The SET PAGE (or SET PTE) command allows you to set fields within
    the page table entry (PTE) for a specific page.  Virtual memory
    must be enabled for this command to work.

    Note that you can specify multiple fields in a single command
    just by giving additional field/value pairs.
    Syntax:
 
        SET PAGE <address>  <field>=<value>  [ <field>=<value>...]

    Parameters:

        <address>     Any expression representing a page of VAX virtual
                      memory.  The PTE selected is the one for the page
                      containing this virtual address.

        <field>       A field from the PTE.  This can be one of:

                          VALID     Is this PTE valid (mapped)?
                          PROT      The protection code
                          M         The modified bit
                          OWN       The owner field
                          S         The software bit field
                          PFN       The page frame number

        <value>       A numeric valid to be assigned to the field given.
                      This can be an expression or a symbol, as shown
                      in the example below.

    Example:

        SET PAGE 200 PROT=PTE$K_KWUR

        This sets the page containing the address 00000200 protection
        field to allow KERNEL writes and reads by any level.  Note that
        the standard PTE protection mnemonics are predefined for you.

$SHOW,SHIM
$SH  ,SHIM
    The SHOW SHIMS command displays the defined runtime library shims
    that have been defined.  Shims take the place of real sharable images
    when running a VAX/VMS image with the RUN command.  These represent
    runtime library offsets that are remapped to real routines in the
    micro-kernel.  These routines may implement the runtime using native
    VAX code, or may use a special XFC dispatch code of XFC$SHIM to 
    transfer control to a runtime support routine in the emulator.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW SHIM
        
$SHOW,TB  
$SH  ,TB  
    The SHOW TB command will display the current contents of the virtual
    memory translation buffer.  This buffer holds recent translations of
    virtual to physical addresses.  The display will show the efficiency
    of the buffer in terms of translation attempts that were cached.  It
    also shows how many times the buffer was flushed or the protection
    data was flushed.  When a mode change occurs in the VAX, the buffer
    discards it's understanding of the page protections, so they must
    be re-tested the next time a buffer translation is used.

    The SHOW TB command will display the virtual address of each page
    in the translation buffer, it's current physical page mapping, the
    current protection on that page, and whether or not it is valid
    for the current mode or not.

    Syntax:

        SHOW TB

$SHOW,MODE
$SH  ,MODE

    The SHOW MODE command displays the current processor mode.  This
    will be one of USER, SUPER, EXEC, or KERNEL.  The mode is stored
    in the CUR_MOD field of the PSL.

    Syntax:

        SHOW MODE

$SET ,MODE

    The SET MODE command sets the current processor mode in the PSL.
    This is equivalent to using the SET PSL command to set the CUR_MOD
    field.

    Note that in general the console commands respect the mode settings
    for the emulated CPU.  For example, if you are in USER mode, the
    console EXAM command will not allow you to read memory that is not
    accessible from USER mode.  You can use the SET MODE command to
    temporarily modify the processor mode so that you can directly
    manipulate privileged memory or registers.

    Syntax:

        SET MODE <modename>

    Parameter:

        <modename>     This is the name of the mode you wish to set
                       the processor to.  It can be one of USER, SUPER,
                       EXEC, or KERNEL.

$HELP

    govax a VAX software emulator.  It supports execution of a subset
    of the VAX instruction set.  It also contains emulation of VAX hardware,
    memory management, and console functions.  The console functions are
    extended beyond existing VAX implementation to include an assembler, 
    disassembler, and debugging tools like breakpoints.

    You can get help on a topic by using the HELP command, followed by
    one or more keywords.  For example, to get help on the SHOW command,
    issue the command:

           HELP SHOW

    To get help on the SHOW REGISTERS subcommand to SHOW, use the command:

           HELP SHOW REGISTERS



    For additional information, see the help on the following command topics:

    CALL            Execute instructions by calling into a procedure
    CLEAR           Clear memory, symbols, etc.
    DISASSEMBLE     Disassemble memory as VAX instructions
    DISMOUNT        Detach a device's mounted disk-image container
    DO              Assemble and execute a single instruction
    EXAMINE         Examine the contents of memory
    GO              Execute VAX instructions
    INCLUDE         Execute a text file of console commands
    INITIALIZE      Initialize the virtual VAX
    LOAD            Load a saved memory image
    MOUNT           Attach a disk-image container file to a device
    QUIT            Quit the virtual VAX
    SAVE            Save the machine state
    RUN             Load and run a VAX/VMS image
    STEP            Singlestep VAX instructions
    SET             Set the state of the console or VAX
    SHOW            Display the state of the console or VAX
    VMINIT          Initialize a VM map
    

$DO  
$.  
    The DO command (which can be abbreviated to a single period ".") takes
    a single VAX instruction, assembles it into scratch memory, and executes
    it.  This can be used to manipulate processor registers, etc.  The
    instruction is assembled into a page of system memory identified by the
    symbol CONSOLE$SCRATCH, normally set up by the VMINIT command.  The
    command is executed in single step mode, so only a single instruction
    will be executed even if it is a branch or causes an exception.

    Because the PC is restored to it's value before the command is given,
    it cannot be used to change the PC via  branch, etc.  Additionally,
    because it is a single command line, multi-line instructions like CASEx
    should not be used.

    Syntax:

        DO <statement>

    Parameter:

        <statement>   Any valid single line of assembler code.  Note that
                      this can include pseudo-ops if needed.  Note that
                      some statements make no sense in this context, such
                      as declaring a .vector or a .entry label.

    Examples:

        DO MOVL #33, R0     ; Sets R0 to the value 33
        .  INCL  R0         ; Increments the value in R0

$VMIN
$VM  

    The VMINIT command creates a functional virtual memory map that uses all
    of physical memory to create P0, P1, and S0 virtual address spaces.  It
    also initializes the required privileged registers and stack pointers to
    begin using it.  The command will display information about the physical
    and virtual address ranges for each region that it creates.
    
    Syntax:
    
        VMINIT  <region> = <pages> [ <region> = <pages>...]
    
    Parameters:
    
        <region>    This specifies a region that is to be explicitly sized.
                    If a region is explicitly sized, then the command is
                    specifying the number of pages of physical memory to
                    allocate to that region.  If a region is not explicitly
                    sized then unused physical memory is evenly divided
                    among the implicitly sized regions.
                    
                    A region can be one of P0, P1, or S0.
        
        <pages>     This specifies the number of pages to allocate to the
                    region.  This is in the current base, so be careful
                    to not assume decimal numbers, etc.
                    
                    Also note that the S0 space has a minimum size, based
                    on the sizes of the other regions.  This is because the
                    S0 region must contain the PFN database for P0 and P1
                    space, as well as minimal room for the privileged mode
                    stacks.  If you do not specify a large enough S0 space
                    (or there is not enough physical memory left to allocate
                    to it) then an error message is displayed.
                    
$TEST

    The TEST statement executes special debugging sequences in the emulator.
    
    Syntax:
    
        TEST    <test>
    
    Parameter:
    
        <test>      The test number to execute.
        
                    11004   Toggle VM tracing
                    11003   Toggle translation buffer usage
                    11001   Generate instruction.h file

$ASM 
$ASSE
    The ASM command invokes the pseudo assembler.  This can be used to store
    instructions or data in memory.  It recognizes the standard VAX assembler
    syntax.  It can create a symbol table, including forward references.  It
    can also be used to set the attributes of the processor itself.

    The assembly continues until the end of the input file is read or a
    .END pseudo opcode is found.  See the help on ASM PSEUDO for more
    information on supported pseudo-opcodes.
    
    Syntax:

        ASM  [<file>]

    Parameter:

        <file>       You can optionally specify a file on disk that is to
                     be read in and assembled.  If you do not specify a 
                     file then the console prompt changes to ASM> and the
                     assembler statements are entered from the keyboard.

$ASM ,PSEU

    The assembler supports a number of "pseudo-opcodes" which are really
    instructions to the assembler rather than actual VAX opcodes.  You can
    get help on each of the pseudo-opcodes.  To get help, use the command
    HELP ASM PSEUDO followed by the name of the pseudo-opcode.

    Note that in the actual assembler code, pseudo opcodes are identified
    by a leading "." character.  Do not specify this in the help command.

        ALIGN
        ASCIC
        ASCID
        ASCII
        ASCIZ
        BASE
        BLKB
        BLKD
        BLKF
        BLKL
        BLKW
        BYTE
        CASE
        CLEAR
        CONSOLE
        D_FLOAT
        END
        ENTRY
        F_FLOAT
        IF
        INCLUDE
        LONG
        MODE
        PRINT
        PTE
        REGION
        SCB
        SET
        SHIM
        WORD
        VECTOR
        
$ASM ,PSEU,VECT

    The VECTOR directive starts assembling code that is stored in the
    SCB vector.  The address of the code following the directive is
    stored in the SCB slot.

    Syntax:

        .VECTOR  <slot-number> [, <stack-name> ]

    Parameter:

        <slot-number>   This is the number of the slot that will be
                        filled with the address of the following
                        instructions.  The address is aligned on an
                        8-byte boundary automatically.  The slot
                        number is usually expressed symbolically,
                        such as with EXC$ARITH for the arithmentic
                        trap slot.

        <stack-name>    This can be either ISP or KSP.  If omitted,
                        then KSP is assumed.  This indicates if the
                        fault runs on the interrupt stack (with VM
                        turned off) or not.

$ASM ,PSEU,CLEA

    The CLEAR directive will clear all symbols from the user symbol table,
    or a specific symbol.  If no name is given then all symbols will be
    cleared.

    Syntax:

        .CLEAR   [ <symbol-name> ]

$ASM ,PSEU,SET 

    The SET directive sets a symbol in the symbol table.  This can often
    be used in conjunction with expressions containing the "." operator
    which means the current location being assembled into.

    Syntax:

        .SET   <symbol-name> <value>

    Parameters:

        <symbol-name>     The name of the symbol to create or set.

        <value>           Any valid expression.

$ASM ,PSEU,SCB 

    The SCB directive allows you to define a system control block address
    for catching system exceptions.  These are stored in the SCB by the
    assembler, using the current value of the SCBB base register.

    Syntax:

        .SCB  <exception>, <address> [, <stack>]


    Parameters:

        <exception>     This is the exception code.  This must be a
                        multiple of 4.  Usually, it is defined using
                        one of the predefined symbols.  For example,
                        use the symbol EXC$ACCVIO to indicate the
                        Access Violation exception handler slot in 
                        the SCB.

        <address>       This is the address of the exception handler.
                        It must be a quadword aligned address.

        <stack>         If given, this may be ISP or KSP to indicate
                        which stack the handler is to run on.  If
                        omitted, then the Kernel Stack Pointer is
                        assumed.

$ASM ,PSEU,ALIG

    The ALIGN directive advances the current location if needed
    to ensure that it is an even multiple of the alignment value.
    For example, a system exception handler must start on a quad-
    word boundary, so an .ALIGN 8 can be used to ensure that the
    following instruction is sure to have the correct starting
    address.  Note that while the current location may be changed
    by this directive, nothing is written into the memory locations
    that are skipped over.

    Syntax:

        .ALIGN  <expression>

    Parameter:

        <expression>     An integer expression, indicating the
                         multiple that the current location must
                         match.  Typically, values of 2, 4, or 8
                         are most often used for word, longword,
                         and quadword boundaries.

$ASM ,PSEU,REGI

    The REGION pseudo opcode switches between S0 and P0 address 
    spaces.  The assembler maintains two "current locations",
    one for each of the code regions.  The .BASE directive can
    be used to set the current location for the current region.
    Using the REGION directive selects which current locations
    is to be used.

    After initialization, the P0 base starts at address 00000200
    and the S0 region starts at the first page following the SCB
    and page tables.

    Syntax:

        .REGION  <region>

    Parameter:

        <region>    The region to assemble instructions into. 
                    This can be P0, 0, or PROCESS for the P0
                    region, or S0, 2, or SYSTEM for the S0
                    region.

$ASM ,PSEU,PRIN

    The PRINT opcode is used to display messages during assembly of
    a source file, such as version or copyright information.  This is
    equivalent to the PRINT command and has the same parameters.

    Syntax:

        .PRINT    <expression>  [, <expression> ]

$ASM ,PSEU,BASE

    The BASE opcode is used to set the address (physical or virtual,
    depending on the MAPEN register setting) to begin storing the 
    next instructions or data.  This is equivalent to the console
    command SET BASE.

    Syntax:

        .BASE    <expression>

    Parameter:

        <expression>     Any valid integer expression.  This defines
                         the location where the next instruction or
                         data will be written.

$ASM ,PSEU,LONG

    The BYTE, WORD, and LONG pseudo-opcodes store specific given values
    of the indicated size in memory.

    Syntax:

        .BYTE    <data> [, <data> ]

        .WORD    <data> [, <data> ]

        .LONG    <data> [, <data> ]


     Parameter:

        <data>     The data value(s) to be stored.  If more than one
                   is given, they must be separated by commas.  The
                   data is stored in memory in the order given.

$ASM ,PSEU,ENTR

    The ENTRY opcode defines a procedure entry point.  It can be followed
    by an optional register mask specification.  The symbol is stored as
    an entry mask label, and a mask word is generated.

    Syntax:

        .ENTRY    <name> [, <mask> ]

    Parameter:

        <name>    The required name of the entry point.  This may be
                  used as a label in a CALLS or CALLG instruction, or
                  as the entry for a CALL console command.

        <mask>    If specified, this determines the register mask to
                  associate with the procedure.  If ommitted, then a
                  mask of zero (no saved registers) is assumed.  The
                  mask is usually specified as ^m<r2,r3> syntax.

$ASM ,PSEU,END 

    The END opcode terminates the assembly.  If assembling from the
    console terminal, it returns the prompt to the normal VAX> prompt.

    Syntax:

        .END    [<entry>]

    Parameter:

        <entry>        If specified, it indicates the starting entry
                       point for execution.  The symbol __ENTRY is set
                       to this value (which can be any valid address
                       expression).  A CALL instruction is generated to
                       this address to start the program.

                       If you do not want to auto-execute an assembled
                       program, do not include an <entry> value on the
                       END statement.

$ASM ,PSEU,BLKB
$ASM ,PSEU,BLKW
$ASM ,PSEU,BLKL

    The block storage opcodes BLKB, BLKL, and BLKW create a block of
    zeros of byte, longword, or word length.

    Syntax:

        .BLKB      <count>        Creates <count> bytes of zeros.

        .BLKW      <count>        Creates <count> 16-bit words of zeros.

        .BLKL      <count>        Creates <count> 32-bit longwords of 
                                  zeros.

    Parameter:

        <count>        The number of elements to write to the storage.
                       The actual number of bytes is <count> * <size>
                       where <size> is specified by the opcode (byte,
                       word, or longword).

$ASM ,PSEU,ASCI

    The character string storage pseudo-opcodes ASCIC, ASCID, ASCII, and
    ASCIZ all store a string in memory.  The differences are in the format
    of the string storage.

    Syntax:

        .ASCIC     "<string>"      Stores a counted ASCII string.  The
                                   storage contains a 16-bit word that
                                   describes the length of <string>,
                                   followed by the bytes of the string.

        .ASCID     "<string>"      Stores a string descriptor.  The first
                                   eight bytes are a standard VMS string
                                   descriptor, followed by the actual bytes
                                   of the string.  The descriptor is
                                   initialized with the length and address
                                   of the string data.

        .ASCII     "<string>"      The bytes of the string data are stored
                                   in memory.

        .ASCIZ     "<string>"      A null terminated string is stored.  The
                                   bytes of <string> are written, followed
                                   by an additional byte containing a zero.

$ASM ,PSEU,PTE 

    The .PTE pseudo operator creates a page table entry longword at the current
    location in memory.

$ASM ,PSEU,CASE

    The .CASE pseudo operator is used to construct the branch table that
    follows a CASEB, CASEW, or CASEL instruction.  The branch table must
    be stored in memory immediately following the CASEx instruction.  Each
    element of the branch table is a displacement address of where the
    CASEx will transfer to based on the selector operand.

    This is difficult to code without the .CASE since the table must be
    a set of word displacements from the start of the table.  With the
    .CASE pseudo operator you can simply express it as a list of
    labeled destinations that can be forward references if needed.

    Syntax:

        CASEx selector.rx, base.rx, limit.rx

        .CASE    dest1 [, dest2...]

    Parameters:

        The above syntax specifies that a CASEx opcode must precede the
        .CASE table for it to be meaningful.  This can be followed by one
        or more .CASE pseudo opcodes.  If more than one item is to be
        given for each .CASE then they must be separated by commas.

        Note that an error occurs if the displacement between the label
        and the address of the first .CASE entry cannot be expressed as
        a signed 16-bit word.

    Example:

                  CASEB     (R0), @#BASE_VALUE, #2
                  .CASE     RED
                  .CASE     BLUE
                  .CASE     GREEN
                  MOVL      #ERR_CODE, R0
                  ...
 
        RED:      ADDL2   R2, R3
                  ... 


        In this example, there is a case table of three items.  Note
        that the limit value is zero-based, so for a table of three
        items the range is 0-2 and the limit is 2.  Each .CASE specifies
        the branch destinations in order.  One of these will be chosen
        by the VAX based on the selector in R0, less the base value.
        Note that if the selector value is outside the range allowed
        by the base value and limit, then control resumes after the
        .CASE block with the MOVL of the error code.

        It is an undetected programmer error to specify a .CASE table
        that is not _exactly_ as long as the limit-1 specifies!


$PRIN

    The PRINT command can be used to print messages or expressions.  The 
    text parts of a PRINT command are displayed just as they are shown
    in the quotation marks.  Numeric expressions can be PRINTED as well,
    and are displayed in the current default radix.  If the radix is
    decimal, then they are printed as decimal numbers without leading
    spaces or zeroes.  If the radix is hexadecimal, the values are
    printed as 8-byte hexadecimal values.

    Syntax:

        PRINT <expression> [, <expression>...]

    Parameters:

        <expression>    One or more expressions or quoted string values,
                        separated by commas.

$AUTH
$ABOU

